{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Both $36$ and $x^2$ are *perfect squares,* since $36=(\\blueD{6})^2$ and $x^2=(\\greenD{x})^2$.\n\n$36-x^2= (\\blueD{6})^2-(\\greenD{x})^2$\n\nSo we can use the *difference of squares* pattern to factor.\n\n$\\blueD{a}^2 - \\greenD{b}^2 =(\\blueD{a}+\\greenD{b})(\\blueD{a}-\\greenD{b})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In this case, $\\blueD{a}=\\blueD{6}$ and $\\greenD{b}=\\greenD{x}$:\n\n$(\\blueD{6})^2 - (\\greenD{x})^2 =(\\blueD{6}+\\greenD{x})(\\blueD{6}-\\greenD{x})$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion,\n\n$36-x^2=(6+x)(6-x)$\n\n*Remember that you can always check your factorization by expanding it.*","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Factor as the product of two binomials.**\n\n$36-x^2=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":0,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"\\left(6+x\\right)\\left(6-x\\right)"},{"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"buttonsVisible":"focused","considered":"correct","form":true,"functions":["f","g","h"],"key":1,"simplify":false,"times":false,"value":"-\\left(x+6\\right)\\left(x-6\\right)"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"static":false,"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
